---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Zhambon: four roles, one codebase, no open ports"
  description="An investment-pool platform where investor, trader, accountant and admin read the same numbers, served from an origin with no inbound ports."
  path="/work/zhambon"
>
  <a class="back" href="/">Index</a>

  <h1 class="lede">Four roles, one codebase</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    An investment-pool platform. Investors, traders, accountants and admins see the same numbers
    but can do very different things with them. The server has no inbound ports open.
  </p>

  <dl class="dossier">
    <div>
      <dt>Surface</dt>
      <dd>Investor, trader, accountant and admin, in one installable app</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Work</dt>
      <dd>Front-end architecture, deployment topology, delivery pipeline</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Stack</dt>
      <dd>Angular PWA, Caddy, Docker Compose, Cloudflare Tunnel, NestJS, Postgres</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Split by role, not by layer</h2>
    <p>
      Grouping a four-role app by layer works until you have to trace what a trader may see through
      six folders that each know a little about everyone. So each role owns its own features,
      services, stores and routes. Roles don't import from each other. Anything two of them share
      gets moved into shared code on purpose, so "who can see this number" has one place to look.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing dials in</h2>
    <p>
      A finance app on a small VPS gets scanned all day once ports 80 and 443 are open. Here they
      aren't. A tunnel connector inside the network dials <em>out</em> to Cloudflare and keeps the
      connection open, so traffic comes in over a channel the server opened. The firewall allows
      SSH and nothing else, and DNS points at the tunnel, never at the server's address.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="diagram">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 800 430" role="img" aria-labelledby="topology-title topology-desc">
      <title id="topology-title">Deployment topology for zhambon.com</title>
      <desc id="topology-desc">
        A browser reaches the Cloudflare edge, which carries traffic through a tunnel that the
        origin opened outward. Inside the server, a tunnel connector passes requests to Caddy, which
        serves the static app and proxies API calls to the NestJS server, which talks to Postgres,
        Redis and Ollama. The firewall permits SSH only.
      </desc>

      <g class="d-box">
        <rect x="320" y="8" width="160" height="40" rx="2"></rect>
        <rect x="160" y="80" width="480" height="58" rx="2"></rect>
        <rect x="80" y="292" width="130" height="48" rx="2"></rect>
        <rect x="248" y="292" width="110" height="48" rx="2"></rect>
        <rect x="400" y="268" width="150" height="44" rx="2"></rect>
        <rect x="400" y="342" width="150" height="44" rx="2"></rect>
        <rect x="590" y="268" width="150" height="44" rx="2"></rect>
      </g>

      <g class="d-frame">
        <rect x="40" y="200" width="720" height="222" rx="2"></rect>
        <rect x="60" y="254" width="680" height="152" rx="2"></rect>
      </g>

      <g class="d-line">
        <path d="M400 48 L400 76"></path>
        <path d="M210 316 L242 316"></path>
        <path d="M358 308 L396 292"></path>
        <path d="M358 326 L396 360"></path>
        <path d="M550 290 L586 290"></path>
      </g>

      <g class="d-tunnel">
        <path d="M400 290 L400 142"></path>
      </g>

      <g class="d-label">
        <text x="400" y="33" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text>
        <text x="400" y="107" text-anchor="middle" class="d-strong">Cloudflare edge</text>
        <text x="400" y="125" text-anchor="middle">TLS, cache rules, rate limiting, bot filtering</text>

        <text x="420" y="196">tunnel opened from the inside</text>
        <text x="420" y="214">no inbound ports, origin address hidden</text>

        <text x="56" y="224" class="d-strong">Origin server</text>
        <text x="56" y="242">firewall: SSH only, 80 and 443 closed</text>
        <text x="74" y="274">private container network</text>

        <text x="145" y="321" text-anchor="middle">tunnel connector</text>
        <text x="303" y="321" text-anchor="middle">Caddy</text>
        <text x="475" y="295" text-anchor="middle">API server</text>
        <text x="475" y="369" text-anchor="middle">static app</text>
        <text x="665" y="288" text-anchor="middle">Postgres, Redis,</text>
        <text x="665" y="303" text-anchor="middle">Ollama</text>

        <text x="366" y="288">/api</text>
        <text x="366" y="352">else</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>One origin</h2>
    <p>
      The browser never talks to an API host. The app calls a path on its own origin, and Caddy
      decides whether that path goes to the API or the app. That means no CORS setup, no preflight
      on every call, and no API hostname in the bundle.
    </p>

    <h2>Deploys that leave nothing behind</h2>
    <p>
      A push to main builds an image tagged with the commit. The deploy step logs the server into
      the registry with the job's own short-lived token, updates the container and logs out. The
      token dies with the job, so no long-lived registry credential sits on the server for an
      intruder to find.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p><a class="action" href="/work/remorch-pay">Remorch, where a dependency had to go</a></p>
</Base>

<style>
  .d-box rect {
    fill: var(--panel);
    stroke: var(--rule);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  .d-frame rect {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--rule);
    stroke-width: 1;
    stroke-dasharray: 3 3;
  }

  .d-line path {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--dim);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  .d-tunnel path {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--accent);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    stroke-dasharray: 5 4;
  }

  .d-label text {
    fill: var(--dim);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 11px;
  }

  .d-label .d-strong {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
